title: A circuit that adds
check: behaves
parts: and or not chip:my_xor
needs: my_xor
---- intro
Time to make your circuit do arithmetic. Adding two single bits gives 0, 1 or 2. Written in binary, 2 is **10**, so the answer needs two lights: **sum** (the right digit) and **carry** (the left one).

| a | b | carry | sum |
|---|---|-------|-----|
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 |

This circuit is called a **half adder**. Your XOR from the last lesson is in the list on the left, under **Chips**.
---- task
Build the half adder: light `sum` and `carry` as the table says. Look closely at each column: you have built both of them before.
---- start
input a 0 0
input b 0 80
led sum 320 0
led carry 320 80
---- reference
input a, b
led sum, carry
sum = a ^ b
carry = a & b
---- answer
input a 0 0
input b 0 80
chip x1 my_xor 150 0
and g1 150 80
led sum 320 0
led carry 320 80
a -> x1.a
b -> x1.b
x1.y -> sum
a -> g1.0
b -> g1.1
g1 -> carry
---- hint1
Compare the `sum` column with the XOR table from the last lesson, and the `carry` column with the AND table.
---- hint2
Place your **my_xor** chip and wire `a` and `b` into it and its output to `sum`. Then an AND gate, also fed by `a` and `b`, into `carry`. Both gates share the same two inputs.
---- solution
`sum` is your XOR of `a` and `b`, and `carry` is an AND of them. 1 + 1 gives sum 0 and carry 1: binary 10, which is 2.
---- done
Your circuit adds! Two of these and an OR make a **full adder**, which also adds a carry coming in; put eight of those in a row and you can add 8-bit numbers, which is what the arithmetic part of every processor does. You got here from switches and lights, one gate at a time.
